export const DE_C2_S08_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "de-morgestraich": [
    `At three minutes to four in the morning, on the Monday after Ash Wednesday, Basel is the only city in the world in which a hundred thousand people hold their breath at the same time. They stand packed together in the lanes of the old town, in the February frost, in coats over pyjamas, and nobody speaks, because they are all waiting for the same thing: for the city to put out its lights. Annemarie Lüthi stood on the Spalenberg, wedged between a retired couple from Riehen and a student who smelled of mulled wine, and asked herself for the hundredth time that night what she was actually doing here.`,
    `She had left at twenty-six, after a quarrel whose cause she could no longer have named but whose wording she still knew by heart. Her father, Ruedi Lüthi, typographer, piper in a carnival clique since the age of nine, had said at the end that whoever left Basel was leaving him too, and she had said that suited her fine. Then thirty-two years of Hamburg. Christmas cards, in both directions, with a signature and no message. Last October a call from the cantonal hospital, too late to say anything, but just in time to have said nothing at all.`,
    `At four on the dot the lights went out. The shop windows, the street lamps, the windows of the flats, all at once, and somewhere into the darkness a drum major called out the command she hadn't heard since childhood and which nevertheless lodged inside her again at once like a swallowed cherry stone: "Morgestraich, forward, march!" Then the drums came in, and then the piccolos, high and piercing and a little out of tune, as they should be, and above the heads swayed the big painted lanterns, the only lights in the whole city.`,
    `She had forgotten what it felt like. You don't hear the Morgestraich, she thought, you stand in it as in rain. The cliques marched past, one after another, masked, with huge noses and goggle eyes, and she couldn't have told any of them apart. On one lantern someone had painted the cantonal government as a seal balancing the budget on a ball, and the people around her laughed quietly, as if laughing in a church. Until the moment she heard a particular passage in a particular march.`,
    `It was the "Arabi", an old march every child here knows, and at the point where the melody drops after the second part, one of the piccolos made a little detour upwards, a trill that was in no sheet music. Annemarie knew this trill. Her father had invented it when she was four and played it to her every evening on the edge of her bed, and he had said it was her piece, the Annemarie piece, and whoever played it had to ask her first. She had never heard it again. She had never wanted to hear it again, either. Now she pushed through the crowd, apologised, trod on someone's foot and ran after the lantern as far as the Rümelinsplatz, where the clique stopped.`,
    `The piper who had played the trill was hardly taller than her instrument. When she pushed up her mask, the face of a girl of twelve at most appeared underneath, red with cold and pride. Annemarie asked where she had learned that flourish in the "Arabi". The girl looked at her suspiciously, the way you look at a stranger who asks about trills in the middle of the night. Then she said, in the broadest Basel dialect, that Herr Lüthi had taught her, last winter, at the piping school. He had said it really belonged to someone else. But it had to be kept playing so it wouldn't get lost, in case that person came back one day and looked for it.`,
    `Annemarie Lüthi stood still on the Rümelinsplatz for a while as the carnival went on making its din around her, and thought of thirty-two Christmas cards with no message. Then she asked the girl whether she could play it for her once more, just that one bar. The girl hesitated. "He said," she explained seriously, "whoever wants to hear it has to ask first whether they may." Annemarie laughed, for the first time that night, and it sounded almost like crying. She asked. She was given permission. The trill was exactly as it should be, and a little out of tune.`,
  ],
  "de-inklusion": [
    `When the north-easterly has blown against the coast for three days in late autumn, people on Usedom go down to the beach with nets and torches, before sunrise, and search the seaweed for amber. For forty years, as curator of the mineralogical collection in Greifswald, I smiled at this, and since I've been living in Ückeritz I have done it myself. You seldom find anything, and when you do, it is the size of a fingernail and as ordinary as a cough sweet. That, between ourselves, is the real attraction.`,
    `In November a woman from the village rang my doorbell, holding the hand of a boy of perhaps ten, Jannik, who wordlessly held out his fist to me and then, with the solemnity of a magician, opened it. In it lay a piece of amber, honey-yellow, the size of a walnut, and inside it, so perfectly preserved that you could have counted every bristle on its legs, a mosquito. A dealer in Heringsdorf had offered two thousand euros, said the mother. Was that a fair price? I said I would have to keep the stone overnight first.`,
    `Amber can be tested in a way any child can copy at home, and in another way for which you need forty years of professional experience. It passed the first with flying colours. It floated in saturated salt water, it glowed bluish under the UV lamp, and when I held a heated needle to an inconspicuous spot, it smelled of resin and pine forest and not of chemicals. It was real amber; there was no doubt about that. It did not pass the second test. Under the microscope I saw, hardly wider than a hair, a seam: someone had cut the stone open, hollowed it out, put a mosquito inside and sealed it all up again with fresh synthetic resin. The mosquito turned out to be a fungus gnat, of the kind that sits in every flowerpot today. The only thing about this piece that was forty million years old was the packaging.`,
    `I would have left it at that, had a second child not come in the following weeks, and then a third. A girl from Saxony found a spider, a boy from Bielefeld an ant with one feeler raised, as if in greeting. All the stones came from the same stretch of beach, between the pier and the spa building, all had been found after stormy nights, and all bore that hair-fine seam. Whoever made them knew their craft and, what worried me more, asked nothing for it. A forger who doesn't sell is not a forger. He is an educator, or a believer, or both.`,
    `I lay in wait, as they say, although lying in wait on a November morning on the Baltic mainly means freezing. On the third night after the next storm, at about six, a man came down the beach in oilskins, with a bucket. He walked slowly along the tideline and, at regular intervals, like a farmer sowing, threw something into the seaweed. As he passed me, I recognised him. It was Ralf Dobberstein, the spa director.`,
    `He denied nothing. He invited me for a coffee in his office, which at that hour smelled of stale smoke and brochures, and explained the principle to me with the calm of a man who has practised his arguments in front of the mirror often enough. Guests came in autumn because of the amber. Most found nothing. Those who found nothing didn't come back. The stones were genuine, he bought them wholesale from Kaliningrad, the creatures came from his conservatory, and he had taught himself the embedding from the internet. "I'm not cheating anyone," he said. "I'm just distributing the luck a little more evenly. Here we call that inclusion."`,
    `In my life I have examined many forgeries, some of them in museums that have never forgiven me the truth. I could have reported Dobberstein, and I should have. Instead I asked for three days to think it over and went home, where Jannik's stone still lay on my desk, because I hadn't known how to tell a ten-year-old that his mosquito came from a flowerpot in Heringsdorf.`,
    `The following night I went down to the beach myself, with a matchbox from my own collection. In it lay a piece of amber, unremarkable, cloudy, with a tiny, crushed insect inside that you could only recognise as such with a magnifying glass, forty million years old, without a seam. I put it where Jannik and his mother would be searching the next morning, and felt like Dobberstein as I did so, only worse, because I knew better. Jannik found it. He even found it less beautiful than the other one. I explained to him why it was more precious. Whether he understood, I don't know.`,
    `I didn't report Dobberstein. We came to an arrangement whose details I don't wish to spread out here; it concerns the seam and a very small stamp. Since then, at any rate, I have known that the truth, like amber, sometimes only floats because the water is salty enough.`,
  ],
  "de-fuenf-tage-im-maerz": [
    `Anyone who travelled to the Leipzig Spring Fair in the eighties for a West German publisher learned two things there that weren't covered in any publishing training. First: the books on the stand would disappear, a few every day, and you did well not to notice. Second: the people standing in front of the shelves didn't come to buy, because they couldn't buy anything. They came to read. They read with a hunger and a speed that Hartmut Eckhoff, sales director of a medium-sized Frankfurt literary publisher, didn't know from the West, where people bought books in order not to read them.`,
    `He noticed the woman in 1981. She was in her late twenties, wore a grey anorak that was too thin for the fair's weather, and she didn't read like the others, who leafed hastily and wrote sentences down on scraps of paper. She read a single book, a novel from the autumn list, from the beginning. She read for three hours, standing up, then put it back exactly in its place and left. The next day she came back and read on. On the fifth day, shortly before the fair closed, she shut the novel, stood for a moment longer with her hand on the cover, the way you lay a hand on a person's shoulder, and vanished.`,
    `The following March she was there again. And the year after. One book a year, five days a year. Eckhoff didn't know her name and he didn't ask, because it was known that among the stands there were not only readers but also people who took an interest in readers. A conversation with a West German publisher could fill a file. So they didn't speak. He nodded to her on the first day of the fair, she nodded back, and over the years that was their entire correspondence.`,
    `In the third year he tried to give her the book. He did it in the way that had got around among the West German exhibitors: on the last afternoon he pushed the novel she was just finishing to the edge of the table, so far that it almost fell off, and then pointedly turned his back on her to have a long conversation with a colleague about paper prices. When he turned round, the book lay exactly where he had pushed it. She hadn't taken it. He tried again the next year, and the year after, with the same result. It hurt his feelings more than he would have admitted.`,
    `In March 1989, at the last fair to be held in a country that wasn't aware of the fact, she read a slim volume of short stories, and she read it faster than usual. On the fourth day she had finished. She came up to the table where he was sorting order forms and, for the first time in nine years, said a whole sentence to him: "You can stop turning your back on me, Herr Eckhoff. I won't take them. Stolen books are read too fast." Then she left before he could answer, and he realised that she knew his name, presumably from the badge on his lapel, and that he still didn't know hers.`,
    `The following March she didn't come. The fair was full of people who were suddenly allowed to buy and didn't know what with. Eckhoff searched the shelves for a grey anorak, discreetly at first, then not so discreetly, and found none. He told himself that was a good thing. It meant she could now buy books in a shop like anyone else and no longer had to spend five days a year standing in an exhibition hall to read a single novel. He didn't believe a word of it.`,
    `In the autumn of 1994, among returned copies and sales reps' reports on his desk in Frankfurt, lay the catalogue of a small Leipzig publisher. It announced a debut, a novel about a woman who for nine years reads one book after another in a single place without ever taking one with her. The author's name was Renate Mühlberg. Eckhoff ordered an advance copy, which a sales director never does, and opened it while still at the office. On the third page was the dedication: "For H. E., who turned his back on me every March, so that I can thank him one day."`,
    `He read the book in one evening, standing, by the window, without putting it down once. It had been, he had to admit as a professional, read too fast.`,
  ],
  "de-rede-zur-lage-des-semikolons": [
    `Dear members, dear friends of the semicolon, honoured guests, should there be any guests among us, which in view of the weather and our agenda I doubt: I welcome you to the twenty-seventh annual general meeting of the Society for the Cultivation of the Semicolon, here in the side room of the "White Court" hotel in Kassel, only a few steps from the place where the Brothers Grimm once collected the fairy tales in whose happy ending we as a society still believe.`,
    `I won't pretend to you. The situation is serious; but it is not hopeless. You see, I have just used a semicolon, and on purpose, for a full stop would have been too final and a comma too careless. That, in a single sentence, is the whole nature of the sign to which we have dedicated ourselves: it is the punctuation of people who don't want to decide whether they have finished yet. That has always been held against us as a weakness. I consider it the highest form of courtesy. The semicolon leaves the door open for a thought without throwing it out.`,
    `To the figures. At its founding in 1997 our society had one hundred and forty-eight members, among them two publishers' editors, a retired senior grammar-school teacher and a lady from Göttingen who to this day refuses to answer letters without a semicolon. Today we are twenty-one. Eleven are present. The treasurer sends his apologies; he is, as he writes, "indisposed", and I may note that he has used this fine word correctly, which in these times is a comfort in itself. (Applause)`,
    `The enemy, ladies and gentlemen, is not the comma. The comma is a relative, a somewhat nervous one, admittedly, who butts in everywhere, but a relative. Nor is the enemy the full stop, that petit bourgeois of punctuation who delivers every thought home before dark. The enemy is the text message. In the text message there are no more sentences, there are only run-ups. There are exclamation marks in packs and little dots that lead into the void. Last year I read several thousand of my granddaughter's messages, with her permission and to my horror, and found not a single semicolon. Not one. I asked her why. She said it looked like a winking face, and you don't send something like that to just anyone.`,
    `I confess, ladies and gentlemen, that after this conversation I considered proposing to the meeting today that the society be dissolved. I had already written the speech. It was very dignified, it had three quotations from Jean Paul and an ending at which even the treasurer would have wept, had he come. But then, six weeks ago, a new member joined. It is the first in eleven years. His name is Malte, he is twenty-three years old, he studies computer science here at the university and he is sitting back there, next to the coat stand, trying to make himself invisible. (Laughter)`,
    `Malte has explained something to me that I don't want to keep from you. In the programming languages that run half the world today, the railways, the banks, the pacemakers, an instruction is in very many cases ended with a semicolon. Not with a full stop. With a semicolon. Every day, Malte says, billions of semicolons are written on this planet, more than in the whole history of literature put together, and they stand where they belong: at the end of a thought after which something else is coming. I asked him whether the people who write them also love them. He said they swear when one is missing. Ladies and gentlemen, whoever swears when something is missing loves it. (Lively applause)`,
    `The semicolon, dear friends, has not died out; it has emigrated. It has left the desks of editors and moved into the machines, like so many inhabitants of North Hesse moving to Frankfurt, and from what we hear it is doing well there. I therefore move that our society not be dissolved but renamed, namely "Society for the Cultivation of the Semicolon in All Languages, Including Artificial Ones". I ask for your votes; and I ask Malte to keep the minutes, since he, as we now know, uses the sign most often.`,
    `Thank you for your attention; the coffee is ready. (The motion was carried with ten votes and one abstention. The abstention came from the lady from Göttingen, who stated for the record that she had nothing against machines as long as they didn't write back.)`,
  ],
  "de-das-gipfelbuch": [
    `Florian Bischof was a geographer, thirty-one years old and, by his own estimate, the only person in Vorarlberg being paid to type up handwriting. The Dornbirn section of the Alpine Club had decided to digitise its old summit registers, those plastic-wrapped, water-stained notebooks that lie in tin boxes on the summits and in which everyone who arrives at the top writes their name, the date and occasionally a thought they would have kept to themselves down in the valley. Florian sat in the basement of the club office, surrounded by forty-three cardboard boxes, reading fifty years of mountain happiness, mountain weather and spelling.`,
    `Most of the entries were of numbing uniformity. "Great view!" "Finally at the top!" "Greetings to everyone who reads this." But in the books from the Hoher Freschen he came across something different. Every year on the 15th of August, the Feast of the Assumption, there was an entry from two people who signed only with their initials, A. and M., and next to the initials there was a time every time. The first entry dated from 1974: "A. 2 hrs 10, M. 2 hrs 45. M. claims she was identifying the flowers. They were oxeye daisies. A." Underneath, in a different, rounder hand: "There was arnica too. Those who are fast see nothing. M."`,
    `Florian laid the books side by side and read them like an epistolary novel. In 1977 A. had written "Honeymoon hike!!", with two exclamation marks, and M. underneath: "He was faster anyway. I'm wearing a dress." In 1981 a third abbreviation appeared, "and L., 4 months, in the baby sling", and the times got longer. In the eighties they wrote about the weather and about a dog called Bläsi, who came up with them for the last time in 1989. There were years in which the entries were conspicuously brief, just initials and times, without remarks, and Florian caught himself worrying about a marriage whose partners he didn't even know by name. But they always came. Every year. And every year M. was slower than A., and every year she had an explanation for it, and every explanation was better than the last.`,
    `In 2003, for the first time, there was only one letter. "M. 2 hrs 05." Underneath, after a gap, as if she had hesitated for a long time: "Without you I'm faster. That's the worst of it." Florian sat still in his basement for a while. Then he read on. M. kept coming every year; she no longer wrote about flowers now, but to A., short, dry sentences in the second person, the way you write to someone who will be back in a moment. "Bläsi would have chased the chamois today." "L. got married, to a Swabian, you'd have liked him, he walks too fast as well." The times got longer again. In 2019 it was four hours twenty, the handwriting shaky. After that there was nothing more. Not in 2020, not in 2021, not in 2022.`,
    `Florian typed up the entries, as was his job, and wrote nothing in the column for notes, because there was no category for what he had read. He told himself it was none of his business. Then he looked at the calendar. It was the eleventh of August.`,
    `On the morning of the fifteenth he climbed the Hoher Freschen, early, in the fog, feeling like an intruder in someone else's liturgy. Halfway up, at the bench below the alpine pasture hut, sat a very old woman with two walking poles and a thermos flask, watching him approach. She wore a straw hat with a sprig of daisies on the band. "Grüß Gott," she said. "You walk too fast, don't you." Without quite knowing why, he asked whether she was M. She looked him over, then laughed: she had been ill for three years and wasn't sure she'd make it today. Her daughter had forbidden her to go alone. But her daughter was in Stuttgart.`,
    `They went together. It took a long time. She stopped at every hairpin bend, showed him arnica, carline thistle and a rock on which A. had sat in 1974, claiming he wasn't waiting for her, only admiring the view. At the top, at the tin box, she took the new summit register that Florian himself had carried up in the spring and wrote, very slowly: "M. 4 hrs 50. New record." Then she handed him the pen and told him to write his time next to it. He wrote: "F. 4 hrs 50." She read it, nodded and added one more line underneath, and it was, so it seemed to him, the handwriting of 1974: "With two of us I was always slower. At last again."`,
  ],
};

